00:00:41.656 --> 00:00:43.524
You also may be listening,

00:00:43.524 --> 00:00:44.743
because we're going to
start focusing a little more

00:00:44.743 --> 00:00:46.743
on the podcasts, that's right.

00:00:48.588 --> 00:00:49.922
We're in our office.

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:50.805
Show AJ,

00:00:50.805 --> 00:00:53.172
I don't know if anybody's ever seen AJ,

00:00:53.172 --> 00:00:55.698
Just kidding, AJ.

00:00:56.005 --> 00:00:57.834
Let's get into the show.

00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:00.348
- [Voiceover]
Comstock Brewing asks,

00:01:00.348 --> 00:01:03.330
"Gary, if you were going to
crush it by starting a brewery,

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:04.769
what would you do?"

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:06.662
- Comstock, great question.

00:01:06.662 --> 00:01:08.729
You know, obviously I've
thought a lot about this,

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:12.571
because I always do think
about producing products

00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:13.836
and selling them.

00:01:13.836 --> 00:01:15.600
I come from a retail background,
and a lot of our clients

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.772
are CPGs, consumer packaged good products

00:01:17.772 --> 00:01:19.236
that sell to consumers, and I always say,

00:01:19.236 --> 00:01:20.999
what if I had one, and obviously beer,

00:01:20.999 --> 00:01:23.377
coming from the world I grew up in.

00:01:23.377 --> 00:01:25.596
As a matter-of-fact, when I first 
got into my dad's business,

00:01:25.596 --> 00:01:27.999
that was the real first boom of microbrews

00:01:27.999 --> 00:01:31.273
back in like, 1995, 6, 7, 8.

00:01:31.273 --> 00:01:33.409
It was a huge boom back then,

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:34.639
and I've thought a lot about that.

00:01:34.639 --> 00:01:37.437
Look, I mean, I think the
thing that's going on is,

00:01:37.437 --> 00:01:38.610
a couple different things.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.351
First of all, I'd win locally.

00:01:40.351 --> 00:01:42.209
I think microbrews really
need to focus locally,

00:01:42.209 --> 00:01:44.310
so I would probably go
to every local business

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.575
within a five-mile radius by hand,

00:01:46.575 --> 00:01:49.789
and shake hands and kiss
babies to create relationships,

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:51.943
so that they use--
you know, like,

00:01:51.943 --> 00:01:56.278
there's an insurance company
that has 147 employees

00:01:56.278 --> 00:01:58.287
down the street in Cincinnati,

00:01:58.287 --> 00:01:59.715
and you go there, and you become friends,

00:01:59.715 --> 00:02:02.687
and they use your beer
for their events, right?

00:02:02.687 --> 00:02:04.952
So I think localization really matters.

00:02:04.952 --> 00:02:06.657
Next thing I would really focus on

00:02:06.657 --> 00:02:08.851
would be probably Instagram.

00:02:08.851 --> 00:02:11.417
I would go all-in on creating
a very serious profile

00:02:11.417 --> 00:02:15.538
in Instragram, and then using
Facebook dark post ads to

00:02:15.538 --> 00:02:18.893
drive links from the
Facebook ad to your Instagram

00:02:18.893 --> 00:02:20.252
to get people following
there because I think

00:02:20.252 --> 00:02:23.177
the 21- to 30-year-old demo
is living and breathing

00:02:23.177 --> 00:02:25.069
in that platform, and I
think it matters for you

00:02:25.069 --> 00:02:27.077
to win in that platform.

00:02:27.077 --> 00:02:29.318
Number three, I would really focus on

00:02:29.318 --> 00:02:31.675
getting two to three
states to carry my beer,

00:02:31.675 --> 00:02:35.313
and then build very strong
relationship with the sales team

00:02:35.313 --> 00:02:36.212
of that small distributor,

00:02:36.212 --> 00:02:37.662
because you're a small microbrew,

00:02:37.662 --> 00:02:39.195
you're probably going to be
with a small distributor.

00:02:39.195 --> 00:02:40.437
You don't want to get lost in a big one.

00:02:40.437 --> 00:02:43.687
What I mean by that is the
people that represent Budweiser

00:02:43.687 --> 00:02:45.220
and Sam Adams, and things of that nature,

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.507
your little 500 cases a
year gets lost somewhere

00:02:47.507 --> 00:02:48.378
in the back of the warehouse,

00:02:48.378 --> 00:02:50.107
nobody cares, so you want
to go somewhere small,

00:02:50.107 --> 00:02:52.935
where you're a bigger
fish in that smaller pond.

00:02:52.935 --> 00:02:55.779
I think you pick two or
three strategic markets

00:02:55.779 --> 00:02:58.437
and then I would run
Facebook dark post ads

00:02:58.437 --> 00:03:00.481
and Twitter local ads in that market

00:03:00.481 --> 00:03:02.094
to build up some hype,
so now all of a sudden,

00:03:02.094 --> 00:03:03.684
they're like, oh, your stuff sells,

00:03:03.684 --> 00:03:06.610
so those are some of
the tactics I would do.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.711
- [Voiceover] Dawn asks,

00:03:08.711 --> 00:03:10.673
"Gary, I'm ten years old,

00:03:10.673 --> 00:03:12.983
which one of your books
should I read first

00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:15.393
and when should I read it? I can't decide."

00:03:16.582 --> 00:03:17.905
- Dawn, first of all,
big shout out to you,

00:03:17.905 --> 00:03:21.616
because at ten years old, I
had read zero books in my life,

00:03:21.616 --> 00:03:24.929
so I appreciate you
debating the three books.

00:03:24.929 --> 00:03:26.566
You know, actually for you,

00:03:26.566 --> 00:03:29.027
I'd probably just start with
Jab, Jab, Jab, Right Hook.

00:03:29.027 --> 00:03:31.256
I think the Thank You Economy--

00:03:31.256 --> 00:03:32.939
I think Jab, Jab, Jab,
Right Hook in a lot of ways

00:03:32.939 --> 00:03:37.374
is a modern version of Crush
It! with less kind of oomph,

00:03:37.374 --> 00:03:40.712
and rah-rah and hype, so
if you want to get pumped,

00:03:40.712 --> 00:03:42.943
at your 120 months of existence,

00:03:42.943 --> 00:03:45.684
then Crush It! is interesting.

00:03:45.684 --> 00:03:47.447
I think Thank You Economy
is probably the one that

00:03:47.447 --> 00:03:50.407
I want most in your
veins and your religion,

00:03:50.407 --> 00:03:52.243
so that, I might even save for last,

00:03:52.243 --> 00:03:55.562
but Jab, Jab, Jab, Right
Hook has modern-day execution

00:03:55.562 --> 00:03:56.839
of what Crush It! is.

00:03:56.839 --> 00:03:58.406
You're going to really
understand it better

00:03:58.406 --> 00:03:59.823
than a lot of people in the VaynerNation

00:03:59.823 --> 00:04:02.876
because you're so much in a
place where you're growing up

00:04:02.876 --> 00:04:05.163
native to the digital communication world,

00:04:05.163 --> 00:04:07.549
so I'd go Jab, Jab, Jab,
Right Hook, Crush It,

00:04:07.549 --> 00:04:09.307
Thank You Economy.

00:04:09.813 --> 00:04:11.833
- [Voiceover]
Lean Box Company asks,

00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:14.537
"Gary, how long do you spend
creating a single piece

00:04:14.537 --> 00:04:15.627
of content?

00:04:15.627 --> 00:04:18.803
Do you focus on video because
it's more natural for you?"

00:04:19.111 --> 00:04:22.266
- Lean, this is a great question,
and really ironic timing.

00:04:22.266 --> 00:04:26.790
So, obviously you know,
from 2006 to deep into 2014,

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:29.693
every piece of content that
came out came out from me.

00:04:29.693 --> 00:04:32.084
Then, we started building
this amazing team,

00:04:32.084 --> 00:04:35.625
and so, I assume you're
asking about my content.

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:38.887
I don't think about it at all,

00:04:38.887 --> 00:04:41.290
meaning I have religion
of Thank You Economy,

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.449
Crush It and Jab, Jab, Jab, Right Hook,

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:46.880
and I execute off of that,
clearly comes from me.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:50.781
Weirdly, insanely that we're
doing this question today,

00:04:50.781 --> 00:04:54.136
because last night, I started
using Twitter's new video

00:04:54.136 --> 00:04:57.235
product, where I can reply within app,

00:04:57.235 --> 00:04:59.220
and so I'm replying a lot more with video

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:01.263
and so, when I was putting
out content to put out,

00:05:01.263 --> 00:05:03.631
it was video first in mind,

00:05:03.631 --> 00:05:04.607
because I thought that was the best way

00:05:04.607 --> 00:05:05.942
that I could communicate.

00:05:05.942 --> 00:05:07.730
The reason you're seeing so much more

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:10.122
Medium and GaryVaynerchuk.com
written articles

00:05:10.122 --> 00:05:13.082
is Steve and India helping
me transcribe these things

00:05:13.082 --> 00:05:14.487
into written form.

00:05:14.487 --> 00:05:16.529
So, I think that for me,

00:05:16.529 --> 00:05:17.922
I did think video first,

00:05:17.922 --> 00:05:19.478
but it was hard to execute video first.

00:05:19.478 --> 00:05:21.243
For a while, back in 07, 8, 9,

00:05:21.243 --> 00:05:23.239
I'd had a flip-cam with
me and I would tape stuff,

00:05:23.239 --> 00:05:24.748
kind of that whole process,

00:05:24.748 --> 00:05:27.163
now I can do it right
from the Twitter app.

00:05:27.163 --> 00:05:29.299
They made it available for verified users,

00:05:29.299 --> 00:05:32.796
so if any of you are verified,
you can go check it out.

00:05:32.796 --> 00:05:35.243
And I assume they'll roll
it out for everybody soon.

00:05:36.443 --> 00:05:37.878
So, I don't overthink it.

00:05:37.878 --> 00:05:39.840
I think way too many people overthink it.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.641
We're living in a culture
where kids are growing up

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:42.499
overthinking it.

00:05:42.499 --> 00:05:45.018
Every 14-year-old girl on the planet

00:05:45.018 --> 00:05:48.025
is taking an Instagram photo
that's taking her 47 minutes

00:05:48.025 --> 00:05:49.964
because she needs the lighting just right,

00:05:49.964 --> 00:05:51.206
and then if it doesn't get enough likes

00:05:51.206 --> 00:05:53.666
within the first four
minutes, she's taking it down.

00:05:53.666 --> 00:05:57.300
I have the reverse of that rigor.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.784
Mike and I were working out last night,

00:05:59.784 --> 00:06:01.816
talking about how much I've leaned down

00:06:01.816 --> 00:06:02.756
and all these things,

00:06:02.756 --> 00:06:04.777
and then we were talking
about people commenting,

00:06:04.777 --> 00:06:05.601
and I was like, yeah, of course,

00:06:05.601 --> 00:06:07.179
because I would take literally take

00:06:07.179 --> 00:06:09.222
the most unflattering
pictures of all time,

00:06:09.222 --> 00:06:10.256
I could care less about lighting.

00:06:10.256 --> 00:06:11.034
I don't even understand how--

00:06:11.034 --> 00:06:12.798
I think only this year, I realized,

00:06:12.798 --> 00:06:14.075
oh, the lighting's got to come this way

00:06:14.075 --> 00:06:15.480
versus me being in the light, right?

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:18.463
I really, really don't
care about the angles

00:06:18.463 --> 00:06:19.555
and things of that nature.

00:06:19.555 --> 00:06:21.576
I care about the substance.

00:06:21.576 --> 00:06:23.009
I know I'm pretty.

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:25.005
(people laughing off-screen)

00:06:25.962 --> 00:06:27.135
Keep the laughs in.

00:06:27.135 --> 00:06:29.271
I want everybody to know what
the three of you thought.

00:06:29.271 --> 00:06:31.013
Keep the scoff,

00:06:31.013 --> 00:06:32.808
keep the scoffs in, DRock.

00:06:32.917 --> 00:06:34.050
Next question.

00:06:34.519 --> 00:06:35.830
- [Voiceover] Doc asks,

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:38.593
"In episode 62, you asked
if we were going to submit

00:06:38.593 --> 00:06:40.393
a question via Instagram.

00:06:40.393 --> 00:06:42.854
I said, No, because I
don't have a smartphone

00:06:42.854 --> 00:06:44.700
so I can't get an account.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.800
So I borrowed my daughter's
phone to open an account

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.330
and upload this stupid question.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:53.866
My question is, are you
(beep) happy now, Gary?"

00:06:54.892 --> 00:06:56.471
- Doc, you and I have been
jamming and interacting

00:06:56.471 --> 00:06:58.433
on Twitter for a very long time,

00:06:58.433 --> 00:06:59.791
and the fact that you care enough about me

00:06:59.791 --> 00:07:04.214
and this show, to jump into
new waters is in essence,

00:07:04.214 --> 00:07:07.128
the entire thesis of what
I do for a living, which is

00:07:07.128 --> 00:07:09.311
try to get people to stop
drawing lines in the sand,

00:07:09.311 --> 00:07:11.876
and not be religious and
jump in and do new things,

00:07:11.876 --> 00:07:15.348
and taste them, and then
decide if they're stupid.

00:07:15.348 --> 00:07:17.796
I am (beep) happy,

00:07:17.796 --> 00:07:20.130
but I'm going to be a
hell of a lot happier

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.348
in nine and a half months,
when I go back to your account

00:07:22.348 --> 00:07:23.717
that you just put up,

00:07:23.717 --> 00:07:28.623
and see enormously beautiful
selfie shots from the Doc.

00:07:29.175 --> 00:07:31.670
Question of the day.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:34.212
Very basic question, I'm
actually very curious.

00:07:34.212 --> 00:07:38.032
Rank these four platforms in preference:

00:07:38.032 --> 00:07:40.771
Facebook, Twitter, Snapchat, Instagram.

00:07:40.771 --> 00:07:43.476
In your preference,
how much you like them.

00:07:43.476 --> 00:07:45.867
You keep asking questions, I'll--

00:07:45.867 --> 00:07:47.504
Oh!

00:07:47.954 --> 00:07:50.459
27-12, Patriots.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:52.287
This Sunday, that is my firm prediction.

00:07:52.287 --> 00:07:54.934
I equally do not want that to happen,

00:07:54.934 --> 00:07:56.094
but I did say on this show,

00:07:56.094 --> 00:07:58.092
that I wanted them to stick it in my face

00:07:58.092 --> 00:07:59.415
and hit rock bottom.

00:07:59.415 --> 00:08:01.156
It looks like they're trying to execute.

00:08:01.156 --> 00:08:02.706
But they do cheat.

00:08:05.033 --> 00:08:06.444
And there go all the--

00:08:06.444 --> 00:08:09.167
And then I just lost at
least 1,700 Patriot fans.

00:08:09.167 --> 00:08:10.850
The numbers are going to be down.

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:12.006
That's it, I lost them.

00:08:12.006 --> 00:08:14.120
I like them, I just hate their team.

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:15.721
- [AJ] You don't like them.

00:08:15.721 --> 00:08:16.788
- [Gary] Yeah, I don't like them.

00:08:16.788 --> 00:08:17.984
(girl laughs)

00:08:17.984 --> 00:08:20.260
- [AJ] You know, you
might see Teddy and Ted

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:21.920
this weekend.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:23.603
- [Gary] You might see them.

00:08:23.603 --> 00:08:25.428
Yeah, I'll go high-five them.

00:08:25.428 --> 00:08:27.056
Ted, I'll go say hey to.

00:08:27.056 --> 00:08:27.914
- [AJ] Not Teddy?

00:08:27.914 --> 00:08:28.723
- [Gary] Not Teddy.

00:08:28.723 --> 00:08:29.794
All right, cool.

